body: "## Report and scope\n\nSource: https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/issues/1203\n\nImprove the reported warning-close then already-terminal refusal sequence with an executable recovery command. Existing warn/require policy and atomic structured close evidence are shipped contracts; preserve them. A normal pm update can append missing expected_result and actual_result while keeping the item terminal. Do not imply that force, reopen, or rewriting the original close event is required.\n\n## Reported reproduction\n\n## Summary\n\n`pm close` accepts a close that it simultaneously reports as incomplete, and the obvious next command — re-running `close` with the flags the warning names — is rejected. The warning therefore steers an agent into a blocked retry.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```\n$ pm close pm-linear-tfj7 --author pi-orchestrator --resolution \"...\"\nclose_reason: \"...\"\nwarnings:\n  - \"close_validation_missing_fields:pm-linear-tfj7:expected_result (--expected-result),actual_result (--actual-result)\"\n```\n\nThe item is now closed, and the warning names two flags of `pm close`. Supplying them:\n\n```\n$ pm close pm-linear-tfj7 --author pi-orchestrator \\\n    --resolution \"...\" --expected-result \"...\" --actual-result \"...\"\nError: Command failed\nWhat happened:\n  Item pm-linear-tfj7 is already terminal; use --force to close again.\n```\n\nThe only advertised way forward is `--force`, which reads as an override for re-closing a settled item — not as the way to finish a record the CLI just told you was unfinished.\n\n`pm update` does work and is the correct path, but nothing in either message points to it:\n\n```\n$ pm update pm-linear-tfj7 --author pi-orchestrator --expected-result \"...\" --actual-result \"...\"\nstatus: \"closed\"\nchanged_field_count: 2\n```\n\n## Why this matters for agents\n\nThe warning is well-formed and machine-readable, so an agent will act on it, and the action it implies is the one operation that is refused. The three plausible agent responses are all wrong: retry `close` (rejected), add `--force` (a destructive-sounding escape hatch used for a non-destructive completion), or ignore the warning (leaves the acceptance evidence the governance policy asked for permanently missing).\n\n## Expected\n\nEither:\n\n1. `close` completes the record when it is re-run on a terminal item with only the missing fields — no `--force`, since nothing is being overwritten; or\n2. the warning names the command that actually works, e.g. `pm update <id> --expected-result … --actual-result …`; or\n3. the close is refused up front when `governance.require_close_reason`-style validation is unmet, rather than succeeding and reporting the gap afterwards.\n\nOption 3 is the most consistent with how `require_close_reason` itself behaves — that one *does* fail closed:\n\n```\nError: Close reason text is required because governance.require_close_reason is enabled\n```\n\nTwo adjacent close-validation rules currently fail in opposite directions: one blocks the close, the other lets it through with a warning that cannot then be acted on.\n\n## Environment\n\n@unbrained/pm-cli 2026.9.5, Linux, Node 22.\n\n## Delivery boundary\n\nIntake only in PR #1204. This item remains open and unclaimed; no implementation or reproduction success is asserted. Implement through the public SDK with thin CLI adapters and executable negative controls before closing."
